 is to know “what to look at” in a histogram. If there is one conclusion that we can make here, it is that Hollywood likes to give Oscars to young actresses.","position":{"start":{"line":58,"column":1},"end":{"line":58,"column":1}},"key":"afEAjtii3r"}],"key":"bGvN1onyJB"}],"class":"tip","key":"gSaCc6pbWX"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Describing a Distribution","position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"key":"S5H7y7GUGq"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-describing-a-distribution","label":"Check Your Understanding: Describing a Distribution","html_id":"check-your-understanding-describing-a-distribution","implicit":true,"key":"pNQdsDuB8L"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the Best Actress histogram above to answer the following questions.","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"WKIqtjLjpy"}],"key":"vxEPD4xie0"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-5ab7741f068f0c911cebb1163aadce93.mjs","model":{"question":"How many of the Best Actress winners were younger than 30 when they won?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"8","feedback":"8 counts only the 25-29 interval; don't forget the two winners aged 20-24."},{"correct":true,"text":"10","feedback":"Correct! $2 + 8 = 10$ winners were in the 20-24 and 25-29 intervals."},{"correct":false,"text":"12","feedback":"12 is the count for the 30-34 interval, whose winners were not younger than 30."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Add the frequencies of the intervals that contain only ages below 30.","explanation":""},"key":"WEW5HuMVL8"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-5ab7741f068f0c911cebb1163aadce93.mjs","model":{"question":"Which description best matches the shape of the distribution of winners' ages?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"Roughly symmetric","feedback":"A symmetric distribution would have similar tails on both sides; here the right tail is much longer."},{"correct":true,"text":"Skewed right","feedback":"Correct! The data are concentrated at younger ages with a long tail stretching toward the older ages—skewed right."},{"correct":false,"text":"Skewed left","feedback":"Skewed left would mean most winners were old with a tail of unusually young winners."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Where is the long tail—toward the younger or the older ages?","explanation":""},"key":"OiCqohHycv"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-5ab7741f068f0c911cebb1163aadce93.mjs","model":{"question":"You notice a single observation far from the rest of the data in a histogram. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"Delete it so it doesn't distort the analysis","feedback":"Deleting data just for being unusual can throw away real (and often interesting) information."},{"correct":true,"text":"Investigate it—determine whether it is an error or a genuinely unusual observation","feedback":"Correct! Outliers need further investigation—they may be data-entry errors, or genuine unusual observations that deserve attention."},{"correct":false,"text":"Ignore it, since one observation can't matter much","feedback":"Ignoring the outlier means possibly missing an error or an important discovery in the data."}],"multi":false,"hint":"An outlier is a signal, not necessarily a mistake.","explanation":""},"key":"qmmuRZBf06"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":95,"column":1},"end":{"line":95,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s Summarize","position":{"start":{"line":95,"column":1},"end":{"line":95,"column":1}},"key":"gpPmX5Dulh"}],"identifier":"lets-summarize","label":"Let’s Summarize","html_id":"lets-summarize","implicit":true,"key":"jxVPLSXJr9"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"posit